{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-145979","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1030"},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":40,"relevance_3":23,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-145979","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1030"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1030"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_168597421#5_340982832","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1030","rank":3,"snippet":"and seating charts. I was blindsided by the emotional aspect of teaching—I didn’t know how to handle it. Vicarious trauma affects teachers’ brains in much the same way that it affects their students’: The brain emits a fear response, releasing excessive cortisol and adrenaline that can increase heart rate, blood pressure, and respiration, and release a flood of emotions. This biological response can manifest in mental and physical <cover>symptoms such as anger and headaches, or workplace behaviors like missing meetings, lateness, or avoiding certain students, say experts. Yet many teachers are never explicitly taught how to help students who have experienced trauma, let alone address the toll it takes on their own health and personal lives. We reached out to trauma-informed experts and educators around the country to get their recommendations for in-the-moment coping strategies and preventative measures to help teachers process vicarious trauma. We share their tips </cover>below.\" , \"start_char\": 3410.000000 , \"title\": \"When Students Are Traumatized, Teachers Are Too | Edutopia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.edutopia.org/article/when-students-are-traumatized-teachers-are-too\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_168597421#5_340982832","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":40,"relevance_3":23,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-145979","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1030"},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":40,"relevance_3":23,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-145979","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1030"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1030"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_168597421#6_340984647","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1030","rank":4,"snippet":"Minero The Real Costs of Trauma Talking it Out Building Coping Strategies Establishing Coming Home Rituals \" , \"segment\": \"The brain emits a fear response, releasing excessive cortisol and adrenaline that can increase heart rate, blood pressure, and respiration, and release a flood of emotions. This biological response can manifest in mental and physical <cover>symptoms such as anger and headaches, or workplace behaviors like missing meetings, lateness, or avoiding certain students, say experts. Yet many teachers are never explicitly taught how to help students who have experienced trauma, let alone address the toll it takes on their own health and personal lives. We reached out to trauma-informed experts and educators around the country to get their recommendations for in-the-moment coping strategies and preventative measures to help teachers process vicarious trauma. We share their tips </cover>below. Talking it Out Garcia, now a teacher of eight years, says she’s found that confiding in others—either a therapist, her boyfriend, or colleagues—helps her process students’ trauma and her own emotions. Connecting with colleagues to talk through and process experiences can be invaluable for teachers coping with secondary trauma, according to Micere Keels, an associate ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_168597421#6_340984647","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":1},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":40,"relevance_3":23,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
