{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-145979","model_call":50,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1034"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1034"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_389221717#5_809170733","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1034","rank":2,"snippet":"\"Lifeline’s learnings Ann Evans MAPS, Practice Adviser, Service Support and Development, Lifeline Australia Working as a psychologist often entails exposure to potentially traumatic and distressing material, including clients’ stories. Regular exposure means that there is a significant risk of <cover>vicarious trauma and burnout. There is often an assumption that our training as psychologists inoculates us against these ill effects, however, over time, the cumulative effect of being exposed to traumatic or distressing material can take its toll, making helpers such as psychologists more at risk than the general population. The large amount of literature on compassion fatigue for various helpers also supports this notion (Figley, 1995). Recognising this and taking personal responsibility for self-care is essential for every psychologist. This is important not just for psychologists’ own wellbeing but also so that they are able to perform well in their role (Taylor & Furlonger, 2011; Figley, 1995). Evidence indicates that those who have been experiencing symptoms </cover>of secondary traumatisation or psychological distress, even if these are mild, will have difficulty in utilising their skills as well as those who are not distressed (Beaton & Murphy, 1995; Dunkley & Whelan, 2006). Due to the crisis nature of Lifeline ’s work, Lifeline’"},"time":7},"model_calls":50,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":26,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
